  [1mThree tables.[0m

  - [38;5;153musers[39m — [38;5;153mid[39m, [38;5;153memail[39m (unique), [38;5;153mpassword_hash[39m, [38;5;153mdisplay_name[39m, [38;5;153mcreated_at[39m
  - [38;5;153msessions[39m — [38;5;153mtoken[39m (PK), [38;5;153muser_id[39m, [38;5;153mexpires_at[39m. A DB table rather than signed cookies, so logout and revocation actually
    work.
  - [38;5;153mtasks[39m — [38;5;153mid[39m, [38;5;153mtitle[39m, [38;5;153mdescription[39m, [38;5;153mstatus[39m, [38;5;153mcreator_id[39m, [38;5;153massignee_id[39m (nullable), [38;5;153mcreated_at[39m, [38;5;153mupdated_at[39m

  [1mStatus[0m is a three-value enum: [38;5;153mtodo[39m / [38;5;153min_progress[39m / [38;5;153mdone[39m. Not a boolean, because "someone moved it to in_progress" is
  exactly the kind of change worth notifying about. Not a configurable workflow engine — that's real complexity for a
  payoff you haven't asked for.

  [1mOne deliberate omission, flagged.[0m I am [3mnot[0m building a change/event log in this piece, even though notifications will
  need one. I've thought about it and I'd rather not: right now I'd be guessing at what an event looks like, and a
  guessed-wrong event schema is worse than no event schema. What I [3mwill[0m do is keep every task mutation flowing through a
  single choke point in [38;5;153mroutes/tasks.py[39m — one function that writes the change — so piece #3 has exactly one place to
  add emission. That's the cheap insurance; the speculative table is not.

  [1mTooling from the start[0m (cheapest now, before any code exists). My recommendation: ruff + ruff-format, and pytest with
  a first passing test — both match your documented setup. I'd skip e2e and fuzz/mutation testing at this stage; e2e
  becomes worth it once there's a real UI flow in piece #2, and fuzzing has little to bite on in CRUD handlers. Say if
  you want them anyway.

